 ],
 "literal": "That coming is it not rare? He / \"let us embrace\" said day indeed having neared / has become here, O friend! Seashore grove's / shaking trunks to be covered, west wind thrown / adumbu-creeper-climbing sand dunes spreading over, tall palmyra trees / becoming short, the lord of such a shore, / big words having spoken, mother has known indeed.",
 "translation": "Is his coming not rare, my friend? The day he promised we would embrace has drawn near and is already here. In the seashore grove, the sand dunes thrown up by the west wind, where adumbu creepers climb, have risen so high that they cover the shaking trunks of the tall palmyra trees, making them look short. Because of the big rumors spoken about the lord of such a shore, mother has found out about our love.",
 "modern": "தோழியே! அவர் வருவது அரிதாகிவிட்டது. 'நாம் ஒன்றுசேர்வோம்' என்று அவர் குறிப்பிட்ட நாள் நெருங்கி இப்போது வந்துவிட்டது. கடற்கரைச் சோலையிலே, அசையும் அடிமரங்கள் மறையும்படி கோடைக்காற்று வீசி எறிந்த, அடும்புக்கொடிகள் படர்ந்த மணல்மேடுகள் உயரமாய் வளர, நெடிய பனைமரங்கள் குட்டையாகத் தோன்றும் கடற்கரைத் தலைவனைப் பற்றிப் பலரும் அலர் தூற்றியதால், என் தாய் நம் காதலை அறிந்துவிட்டாள்.",
 "explanation": "The friend (tōḻi) is speaking to the heroine (talaivi) about the hero's delay and the rising gossip in the village. She points out that the day the hero promised to return and marry her has already arrived, yet he is not here. Meanwhile, the mother has found out about their secret relationship due to the loud rumors spreading among the villagers. The poem uses a vivid Neytal (seashore) image where the tall palmyra trees are buried by wind-blown sand dunes, making them appear short. This serves as an implicit metaphor (uḷḷuṟai): just as the tall trees are hidden by the rising sand, the hero's noble reputation and their secret love are being overwhelmed and exposed by the rising tide of public gossip.",
